  \href{https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.94.052331}%
       {doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.94.052331};
  \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05532}{arXiv:1511.05532}.

  \item[\textup{[BGS+26]}]\label{ref:p53-brzic-et-al}
  V. Brzi{\'c}, D. Grinko, M. Studzi{\'n}ski, and M. T. Quintino,
  \lq\lq Optimal Pure State Cloning and Transposition Are Complementary
  Channels,\rq\rq{} arXiv preprint (2026).
  \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23628}{arXiv:2603.23628}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Comment}

No strict example and no equality theorem are known.  Complementation turns
Eq.~\eqref{eq:p53-transpose-degradable} into transpose antidegradability and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:p53-degradable} into ordinary antidegradability.  Consequently,
the source document's transpose-antidegradable separation question is exactly
the same existence problem, not a distinct problem.

